      "text": "# EXL3 Serving Design: A New Engineer's Guide\n\nWelcome to the `vllm-exl3` project. This document explains the architecture, memory model, execution paths, and failure handling of the EXL3 (ExLlamaV3) quantization plugin for vLLM.\n\n## 1. High-Level Overview\n\n`vllm-exl3` is an **out-of-tree vLLM plugin** that enables serving of Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models using **EXL3 trellis-packed weights**. It is **not a quantizer**; it consumes pre-quantized checkpoints and bridges them into vLLM\u2019s execution engine.\n\n### Key Design Principles\n- **Packed Execution**: Weights remain in compressed EXL3 format (K2/K3/K4 bit-widths) during inference. No full FP16 dequantization occurs unless a fallback path is triggered.\n- **Routed MoE Focus**: Optimized for models with routed experts (e.g., GLM-5.3, DeepSeek-V4.1, Qwen3.8).\n- **Native CUDA Kernels**: Uses custom CUDA kernels (`exl3_moe_mixedk`, fat-GEMM) for fused expert execution, with fallbacks to Python loops or ExLlamaV3 reference kernels.\n- **Strict Compatibility**: Requires specific vLLM versions (e.g., `0.29.1rc1` or `0.30.0` audit) and model architectures. It does **not** add missing model classes to vLLM.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Memory Layout\n\nMemory management is critical due to the large size of MoE models and the constraints of single-GPU serving (e.g., RTX 5090, GB10).\n\n### 2.1 Packed Expert Weights\n- **Trellis Format**: Weights are stored in EXL3 trellis packs, which are highly compressed (2\u20134 bits per weight).\n- **Resident vs. Offloaded**:\n  - **Resident**: By default, packed weights reside in GPU VRAM. For example, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next uses ~80 GiB of VRAM for weights + n-gram embeddings.\n  - **UVA Offload (Experimental)**: For models too large for VRAM (e.g., DeepSeek-V4.1 on small GPUs",
